1. PURPOSE
The Disaster Testing, Security Testing & Operational Readiness Architecture establishes the formal testing and readiness framework required to determine whether OneDraft can withstand failures, security events, infrastructure disruptions, workload anomalies and operational incidents while preserving system integrity.
The purpose of this subsystem is to verify that the preceding OneDraft architectures operate correctly under normal, degraded, adversarial and failure conditions.
Operational readiness shall be demonstrated through repeatable testing rather than assumed from architectural design alone.
2. ARCHITECTURAL ROLE
The Disaster Testing, Security Testing & Operational Readiness subsystem provides the verification boundary between architectural implementation and production operation.
It evaluates:
Failure Recovery
Security Controls
Tenant Isolation
Data Integrity
Service Availability
Backup Restoration
Queue Recovery
Infrastructure Recovery
Operational Procedures
Production Readiness
Incident Response
3. TESTING MODEL
OneDraft shall use layered verification.
UNIT TESTING
      ↓
INTEGRATION TESTING
      ↓
CONTRACT TESTING
      ↓
SYSTEM TESTING
      ↓
SECURITY TESTING
      ↓
PERFORMANCE TESTING
      ↓
FAILURE TESTING
      ↓
DISASTER RECOVERY TESTING
      ↓
OPERATIONAL READINESS
Each layer shall verify a distinct class of system behavior.
4. TEST ENVIRONMENTS
Testing shall be separated into controlled environments.
Development
Test
Integration
Staging
Production
Production testing shall use controlled procedures and shall not introduce uncontrolled destructive operations.
5. TEST DATA
Testing shall use deterministic and representative datasets.
Test datasets may include:
Small Projects
Large Projects
Complex Geometry
Multi-Level Buildings
Large Drawing Packages
CAD Imports
BIM Imports
Compliance Rules
Validation Results
Redline Histories
Test data shall not expose customer information unnecessarily.
6. TEST FIXTURES
Reusable test fixtures shall establish known system states.
Fixtures may include:
Organizations
Users
Projects
Models
Drawings
Artifacts
Jobs
Events
Subscriptions
Entitlements
Authorization Policies
Fixtures shall be version-controlled where practical.
7. DETERMINISTIC TESTING
Where deterministic behavior is required, the test environment shall control:
Input Data
Configuration
Model Versions
Code Versions
Rule Versions
Random Seeds
Provider Selection
Time-Dependent Behavior
Deterministic tests shall produce repeatable outcomes.
8. DISASTER TESTING
Disaster testing shall evaluate the system's ability to recover from significant failures.
Scenarios shall include:
Database Failure
Database Corruption
Object Storage Failure
Queue Failure
Worker Failure
API Failure
Network Failure
Service Dependency Failure
Infrastructure Loss
Configuration Failure
Credential Failure
9. DATABASE FAILURE TESTING
Database failure tests shall verify:
Application Detection
Connection Recovery
Transaction Safety
Retry Behavior
Failover
Data Integrity
Recovery Procedures
The system shall not silently continue with inconsistent transactional state.
10. DATABASE RESTORE TESTING
Backup restoration shall be tested periodically.
A restore test shall verify:
BACKUP
  ↓
RESTORE
  ↓
DATABASE VALIDATION
  ↓
SCHEMA VALIDATION
  ↓
DATA INTEGRITY
  ↓
APPLICATION STARTUP
  ↓
FUNCTIONAL VERIFICATION
A backup shall not be considered operationally valid merely because a backup file exists.
11. OBJECT STORAGE FAILURE TESTING
Object storage failure scenarios shall verify:
Artifact Availability
Upload Recovery
Download Recovery
Reference Integrity
Signed Access
Backup Restoration
Artifact Hash Verification
Database metadata shall remain consistent with restored artifacts.
12. QUEUE FAILURE TESTING
Queue failure testing shall verify:
Job Persistence
Message Recovery
Duplicate Handling
Retry Behavior
Dead-Letter Handling
Worker Reconnection
A queue outage shall not silently destroy pending jobs.
13. WORKER FAILURE TESTING
Worker failure shall verify that interrupted jobs are correctly classified and recoverable.
The system shall distinguish:
Pending
Running
Retryable
Failed
Cancelled
Completed
Worker termination shall not create an invalid project state.
14. API FAILURE TESTING
API failure testing shall evaluate:
Instance Failure
Gateway Failure
Service Failure
Timeouts
Connection Resets
Partial Responses
The gateway shall continue routing traffic to healthy service instances where infrastructure permits.
15. NETWORK FAILURE TESTING
Network testing shall evaluate:
Latency
Packet Loss
Connection Failure
Service Partitioning
External Provider Failure
Internal Service Isolation
The system shall fail predictably when network dependencies become unavailable.
16. DEPENDENCY FAILURE TESTING
External and internal dependencies shall be tested independently.
Dependencies may include:
AI Providers
Payment Providers
Object Storage
Email Services
Authentication Providers
External CAD/BIM Services
The failure of an external dependency shall not unnecessarily compromise unrelated OneDraft functionality.
17. AI FAILURE TESTING
Aria and model-provider failures shall include:
Provider Unavailability
Timeout
Malformed Output
Invalid Command
Low Confidence
Provider Switching
Context Construction Failure
Model Response Failure
AI failure shall not directly mutate the Project Model without deterministic command validation.
18. DATA CORRUPTION TESTING
Controlled corruption tests shall verify detection and containment.
Tests may target:
Database Records
Model Artifacts
Drawing Artifacts
Metadata
Event Records
Hashes
Integrity mechanisms shall detect invalid or inconsistent data where designed to do so.
19. TENANT ISOLATION TESTING
Tenant isolation shall be continuously tested.
Tests shall attempt to access another organization's:
Projects
Models
Drawings
Artifacts
Jobs
Billing Records
AI Context
Search Results
Events
Telemetry
Unauthorized access shall be rejected.
20. AUTHORIZATION TESTING
Authorization tests shall cover:
Role Boundaries
Project Membership
Organization Membership
Administrative Access
Privileged Operations
Resource Ownership
Expired Permissions
Removed Membership
Authorization shall be tested independently from frontend visibility.
21. API SECURITY TESTING
API security testing shall evaluate:
Authentication Bypass
Authorization Bypass
Malformed Requests
Parameter Manipulation
Request Smuggling
Replay
Credential Abuse
Rate-Limit Bypass
Payload Abuse
Improper Error Disclosure
22. INPUT SECURITY TESTING
Input validation shall be tested against malformed and unexpected input.
Testing shall include:
Oversized Payloads
Invalid Types
Unexpected Fields
Encoding Errors
Boundary Values
Malformed Files
Invalid Identifiers
Unsupported Formats
Input failures shall terminate safely.
23. INJECTION TESTING
Security testing shall evaluate applicable injection classes.
Examples include:
SQL Injection
Command Injection
Template Injection
Path Traversal
Script Injection
Header Injection
Query Manipulation
The exact applicable test set shall reflect the technologies used by OneDraft.
24. FILE SECURITY TESTING
Imported files shall be tested for:
Malformed Structure
Unexpected Content
Oversized Files
Path Traversal
Unsupported Formats
Corrupt Files
Embedded Threats
File processing shall occur within controlled boundaries.
25. ARTIFACT SECURITY TESTING
Artifact access shall be tested against:
Unauthorized Download
Expired Access
Cross-Tenant Access
Invalid References
Modified Artifacts
Improper Content-Type Handling
Artifact integrity and authorization shall be independently verified.
26. SECRETS SECURITY TESTING
The platform shall verify that secrets are not exposed through:
Logs
Errors
Source Control
API Responses
Telemetry
Build Artifacts
Container Images
Secret rotation procedures shall also be tested.
27. IDENTITY SECURITY TESTING
Identity testing shall cover:
Authentication
Session Expiration
Credential Rotation
Account Lockout
Token Validation
Privilege Changes
Membership Removal
Security-sensitive identity changes shall take effect within defined propagation limits.
28. SECURITY MONITORING TESTING
Security telemetry shall be tested to ensure relevant events are captured.
Events may include:
Authentication Failures
Authorization Failures
Privilege Changes
Administrative Access
Rate-Limit Violations
Suspicious Requests
Credential Changes
Security Configuration Changes
29. PENETRATION TESTING
Periodic penetration testing shall evaluate the externally exposed OneDraft attack surface.
Testing shall include applicable:
Web Applications
APIs
Authentication
Authorization
Infrastructure
Storage
Network Services
Findings shall be classified by severity and tracked through remediation.
30. DEPENDENCY SECURITY TESTING
Software dependencies shall be assessed for known vulnerabilities.
Testing shall cover:
Python Packages
TypeScript/JavaScript Packages
Container Images
Operating System Packages
Database Components
Infrastructure Components
Dependency vulnerabilities shall be evaluated according to exploitability and system exposure.
31. CONTAINER SECURITY
Container images shall be tested for:
Known Vulnerabilities
Unnecessary Packages
Excessive Privileges
Unsafe Configuration
Secret Exposure
Untrusted Base Images
Production containers shall use controlled and versioned images.
32. INFRASTRUCTURE SECURITY
Infrastructure testing shall verify:
Network Segmentation
Firewall Rules
Service Exposure
Credential Controls
Storage Permissions
Database Access
Administrative Interfaces
Only required services shall be exposed.
33. DISASTER RECOVERY OBJECTIVES
The disaster recovery architecture shall validate:
RPO — Recovery Point Objective
RTO — Recovery Time Objective
Each critical subsystem shall have defined recovery objectives.
Recovery objectives shall be measurable through actual restoration testing.
34. RECOVERY TESTING
Recovery testing shall verify:
Detection
Isolation
Restoration
Reconnection
Data Integrity
Service Startup
Functional Verification
User Access
A recovered environment shall not be considered operational until functional verification succeeds.
35. FAILOVER TESTING
Where redundant infrastructure exists, failover shall be tested.
Failover tests may include:
Database Failover
Gateway Failover
Worker Failover
Service Instance Failover
Storage Failover
Failover shall preserve data integrity and authorization boundaries.
36. BACKUP VERIFICATION
Backup systems shall verify:
Backup Completion
Backup Integrity
Backup Encryption
Retention
Restore Capability
Historical Availability
Backup monitoring shall detect failed or incomplete backup operations.
37. CHAOS TESTING
Controlled chaos testing may introduce limited failures into non-production environments.
Scenarios may include:
Service Termination
Worker Termination
Network Latency
Queue Interruption
Database Connection Loss
Dependency Failure
Chaos tests shall have defined scope, expected outcomes and termination conditions.
38. INCIDENT SIMULATION
Operational teams shall periodically simulate incidents.
Exercises may include:
Database Outage
Security Breach
Credential Compromise
AI Provider Outage
Storage Failure
Queue Failure
Tenant Isolation Event
Incident simulations shall test both technical recovery and operational coordination.
39. INCIDENT RESPONSE
OneDraft shall maintain documented incident-response procedures.
The response lifecycle shall include:
DETECT
  ↓
CLASSIFY
  ↓
CONTAIN
  ↓
INVESTIGATE
  ↓
RECOVER
  ↓
VERIFY
  ↓
DOCUMENT
  ↓
IMPROVE
Incident response shall preserve relevant evidence and audit records.
40. OPERATIONAL RUNBOOKS
Critical services shall have operational runbooks.
Runbooks shall address:
Startup
Shutdown
Failure Detection
Recovery
Rollback
Database Restore
Queue Recovery
Credential Rotation
Service Isolation
Escalation
Runbooks shall be version-controlled.
41. PRODUCTION READINESS
A subsystem shall not be considered production-ready until required readiness criteria are satisfied.
Criteria shall include:
Automated Tests
Security Tests
Performance Tests
Backup Verification
Recovery Testing
Observability
Alerting
Documentation
Rollback Procedures
Operational Ownership
42. RELEASE READINESS GATES
Production releases shall pass defined gates.
BUILD
 ↓
UNIT TESTS
 ↓
INTEGRATION TESTS
 ↓
CONTRACT TESTS
 ↓
SECURITY TESTS
 ↓
PERFORMANCE TESTS
 ↓
MIGRATION TESTS
 ↓
STAGING VALIDATION
 ↓
OPERATIONAL APPROVAL
 ↓
RELEASE
A failed mandatory gate shall prevent release unless explicitly overridden through controlled administrative procedures.
43. ROLLBACK TESTING
Release rollback procedures shall be tested.
Testing shall include:
Application Rollback
Database Migration Recovery
Configuration Rollback
Container Rollback
Artifact Compatibility
Rollback shall not create incompatible Project Model or database states.
44. MIGRATION TESTING
Database migrations shall be tested against representative production-scale datasets.
Migration testing shall verify:
Forward Migration
Rollback Capability, where supported.
Data Integrity
Performance
Lock Duration
Application Compatibility
Migrations shall be treated as production-critical operations.
45. OPERATIONAL ALERTING
Critical conditions shall produce actionable alerts.
Alerts may include:
Service Unavailability
Database Failure
Queue Backlog
Worker Failure
High Error Rate
Security Event
Capacity Exhaustion
Backup Failure
Recovery Failure
Alerts shall identify the affected subsystem and appropriate response procedure.
46. OPERATIONAL OWNERSHIP
Every production subsystem shall have defined operational ownership.
Ownership shall identify:
Responsible Team
Escalation Path
Service Owner
Recovery Procedure
Critical Dependencies
Operational ownership shall remain current as the platform evolves.
47. SECURITY & DISASTER TEST EVIDENCE
Testing shall produce verifiable evidence.
Evidence may include:
Test Results
Logs
Reports
Screenshots
Metrics
Recovery Records
Security Findings
Remediation Records
Approval Records
Test evidence shall be associated with the applicable software, infrastructure and configuration versions.
48. OPERATIONAL READINESS INVARIANTS
The following invariants shall govern the subsystem.
Recoverability: Critical services shall have tested recovery procedures.
Integrity: Recovery shall preserve authoritative data integrity.
Isolation: Failure testing shall preserve tenant boundaries.
Security: Security controls shall be tested rather than assumed.
Traceability: Significant operational actions shall be observable and auditable.
Repeatability: Critical tests shall be repeatable.
Evidence: Readiness decisions shall be supported by verifiable evidence.
Rollback: Production changes shall have tested rollback or recovery procedures.
No Silent Failure: Critical failures shall generate detectable operational signals.
Controlled Testing: Destructive testing shall occur within explicitly controlled boundaries.
49. IMPLEMENTATION REQUIREMENTS
The implementation shall provide:
Automated Test Suites
Security Test Suites
Performance Test Framework
Disaster Recovery Tests
Backup Restore Tests
Failure Injection Framework
Penetration Testing Procedures
Incident Simulations
Operational Runbooks
Production Readiness Checklist
Release Gates
Security Findings Tracking
Recovery Evidence
Operational Metrics
These capabilities shall integrate with the Testing, CI/CD, Infrastructure, Backup, Observability, Identity, Security and Runtime architectures established by preceding stacks.
50. COMPLETION STATUS
The Disaster Testing, Security Testing & Operational Readiness Architecture establishes the formal verification and production-readiness framework for OneDraft.
The subsystem defines disaster testing, database and storage recovery, queue and worker recovery, AI failure testing, tenant isolation testing, authorization testing, API security, injection testing, file security, secrets protection, identity security, penetration testing, dependency security, container and infrastructure testing, RPO/RTO verification, failover, backup restoration, chaos testing, incident simulation, incident response, operational runbooks, production readiness, release gates, rollback testing, migration testing, alerting, operational ownership and evidence preservation.
The architecture establishes the verification layer required to demonstrate that OneDraft is not merely architecturally complete, but capable of being tested, recovered, secured and operated under real production conditions.
The next architectural layer establishes the Developer Platform, SDK & Extension Architecture, defining how developers and approved external systems interact with OneDraft through supported clients, SDKs, plugins, APIs and extensibility mechanisms.
